With the release of the RTX-30 series, I believe many people are looking for a lightweight, high-performance gaming laptop. While there was a time when gaming laptops were exclusively for students who wanted to play games, it's no exaggeration to say that demand has exploded recently with the increase in VR/AI development and business. For these reasons, I also wanted a lightweight gaming laptop for both business trips and software development. After setting a few conditions and waiting eagerly through Danawa searches, I'm happy to see suitable laptops appearing these days. My conditions for choosing a laptop are as follows:** - RTX-2060 or higher, USB-PD type support, under 2Kg** The reason is that VR-Ready requires at least those specs, and since I travel frequently, a lightweight laptop is essential. Naturally, the laptops I found through my search were MSI's M Stealth series and ASUS's ROG series.

So, the model I purchased is below.

http://prod.danawa.com/info/?pcode=13455101&keyword=MSI+%EC%8A%A4%ED%85%94%EC%8A%A4&cate=112758
                                [**[Danawa] MSI Stealth 15M A11UEK Carbon Gray (SSD 512GB)** Lowest Price 0 won prod.danawa.com](http://prod.danawa.com/info/?pcode=13455101&keyword=MSI+%EC%8A%A4%ED%85%94%EC%8A%A4&cate=112758)

I think I bought it for around 2 million won, but the price has gone up, and these days it's a rare item, hard to even find in stock. I was quite satisfied, as it came with a 100,000 won Steam code. And, happy that it supported PD charging, I took my NoteKing adapter, which I used with my previous Samsung laptop and MacBook, on a business trip. However, while working on the KTX, the power suddenly went out and wouldn't turn back on. First, I experienced the inconvenience of having to work on a backup laptop at the office during my business trip. Fortunately, I always have many important work files, and they were synced to OneDrive, so I could work with relatively little interruption. I just thought it was a divine command on the KTX. I was a bit annoyed that a new product had issues so quickly, but anyway, after returning to Seoul, I visited the MSI Yongsan AS Center.

