It was close to my university, and as someone who enjoyed visiting Yongsan as a child, I went there quite casually. While waiting for the AS registration and inspection, I hastily conducted an online team meeting using a laptop provided in the AS center's lounge. However, after the inspection, the AS technician spoke to me rather coldly. He said, 'It was discharged, and the CMOS was corrupted. I revived it, and it's working fine now.' And without me even asking, he mentioned that future repairs would be charged... Fortunately, my data was intact, so I acknowledged it and prepared to leave. But what was a bit strange was that if it had just been turned on, the battery level shouldn't have been very low. Anyway, it wasn't more important than work, so I didn't dwell on it. However, a similar situation occurred again. This time, I visited again and brought the PD type charger with me. But he said the problem was that it wasn't a genuine charger. And that the PD type charger *must* be plugged into *this specific port*. Then, he added, 'Now that I look at it, it doesn't seem to have been subjected to impact, but there's a significant gap, so I'll order the part and replace it for free,' as if doing me a favor. Since Windows 10 showed normal charging even when the PD type charger was plugged into other ports, it felt strange that he was insisting on only one port, and I found it puzzling and annoying that this was considered a customer issue. But I just said 'okay' and asked for another genuine charger. I bought one on the spot, planning to keep one at the office and one at home, thinking it would be fine if I just used the designated port occasionally. Just in case, I tried using only the genuine standard charging cable, and it worked perfectly for a week. So, this time, when I used the PD type charger in the designated port again, freezing occurred once every 1-2 days. The frustrating thing about freezing is that it stops your work completely. I almost lost a proposal during late-night work once. It was a truly terrifying experience, but fortunately, I managed to recover it from the temporary folder. It became almost certain that it was a PD type charging issue, so I called, and a different employee answered, telling me the part was at the center and I could come anytime. When I explained the PD type issue, they said that if I could provide a video recording showing the power status or indicator lights during freezing, it would help diagnose the problem. So, anticipating a long repair time, I bought a new ASUS-ROG laptop, waited until freezing occurred again to secure the video, then called the next day to arrange a visit. But... that male AS technician started to get on my nerves again. I don't understand why a customer has to listen to complaints about being late, especially after I called. If it was an issue, they could have just sent it. I told him I brought the video recording as requested over the phone. He then asked me to send it via email, so I sent it on the spot. And then he started harping on about genuine adapters again. However, the problem is that MSI does not sell PD chargers. Meaning there is no genuine one. And it was hard to logically understand how a PD charger could damage a laptop unless it was wildly erratic like being struck by lightning. Especially since it was a charger that worked perfectly with Apple and Samsung laptops. Even when I, a busy person, told him I majored in computer science, he kept insisting on genuine products. I honestly felt like throwing the laptop right there and walking out, but I decided against it, not wanting to be labeled a 'difficult customer.' So, I just asked him to test it thoroughly, replace the part, and send it to my office, then left. A few days later, I heard that the laptop had arrived at the office. However, the decisive reason I'm writing this post is that I was so furious to hear that he repeated that nonsense to my colleague.

​ **They say that if you don't use a genuine charging adapter, your laptop can break. That is the official stance of the MSI AS Center. But MSI does not sell PD type charging adapters. Is it true that MSI laptops support USB-C PD charging? If translated into pseudo-code, it would be as follows. Is the ChargeUsbC function correct code?**

```
Charger[] MSIofficialCtypeCharger = null;

function ChargeUsbC(Charger inputCharger){
  if !isOfficalCharger(input) {
     throws Exception(Error);
  }
  return Charging(input);
}

function isOfficialCharger(Charger inputCharger){
  foreach (MSIofficialCtypeCharger  as charger){
     if (charger == inputCharger) return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

And as I used ASUS-ROG, comparing the two units, I'll first write down the presumed reason for the malfunction. I don't know if that technician knows, doesn't know, or doesn't want to know why I have to do QA. First, when I plugged a PD-type charger into the ASUS-ROG, it didn't work at all, and even if it did, it displayed a warning message that it might not charge properly due to low voltage. We had two more PD-type chargers at home besides the NoteKing. Ultimately, what I learned based on this was that PD-type chargers that don't provide sufficient power either don't charge the ASUS-ROG at all or display a warning message. This was new to me, as I hadn't known it when using MSI. Thinking about it, I seem to have had a similar experience with the Galaxy Book when plugging in a PD-type USB-C fast charger, and considering the characteristics of gaming laptops whose original genuine charging adapters are 100W or more, I concluded that low-voltage PD-type charging might not be normal. Since then, I purchased a 100W PD charger and cable for the ASUS-ROG laptop and have been using it well without a single freeze to date. **To be sure, I could ask an MSI employee to try using a 100W PD charger, but since we have no reason to do QA for them, I'm only providing a speculative diagnosis. The Zephyrus currently on sale at Coupang has an RTX-2060, but it also has a Ryzen 9 CPU, 1TB storage, and QHD display. The price is even good. The only drawback is the lack of a webcam, but you can buy one for 35,000 won. I bought one and am using it well. I don't think I'll ever buy an MSI laptop again, nor will I buy one for my employees. Thanks to the MSI employee, I discovered a great ASUS product, so I'm grateful. Additionally, if you are purchasing a laptop for business or software development purposes, please exclude MSI.**

https://www.coupang.com/vp/products/4362229995?itemId=5131708228&vendorItemId=72441116157&pickType=COU_PICK&q=asus+ryzen9+rtx2060&itemsCount=28&searchId=b3db2fa83d934ce08c36a55b4f36ced4&rank=0&isAddedCart=
[**ASUS Zephyrus Eclipse Gray Laptop GA401IV-HA033 (Ryzen 9 4900HS 35.5cm RTX 2060)** COUPANG www.coupang.com](https://www.coupang.com/vp/products/4362229995?itemId=5131708228&vendorItemId=72441116157&pickType=COU_PICK&q=asus+ryzen9+rtx2060&itemsCount=28&searchId=b3db2fa83d934ce08c36a55b4f36ced4&rank=0&isAddedCart=)

It would be great if Samsung and LG could make similar lightweight laptops with enhanced 3D performance for business purposes, even if they raise the price. Lately, there are many people around me buying laptops for AI and VR. It seems MSI thinks its customers are just high school and college students without money, buying laptops only for gaming. In a world where developer labor costs are skyrocketing, if a few freezes occur, they don't seem to realize that the developer's labor cost is far more expensive than the laptop itself. When I went to the MSI laptop center, it said something about protecting employees... I wonder if I'm not the only one who had a bad experience. Additionally, I received a response from an engineer in Yongsan that it doesn't support PD, so I've inquired with the Taiwan headquarters to see if that's correct. I'll wait and see what kind of response I get. --UPDATE: 2021.06.24 I received a call from an engineer, not the employee who handled the AS. The confirmed information was that there shouldn't be an issue with the 65W NoteKing low power, and while 100W hasn't been tested, it seems okay, but PD-type is only recommended for power backup. They apologized that the AS employee had misinformed me and said they would re-educate them.

Our biggest long-cherished project this year, entering the Chinese market, is set to officially commence. This is being realized based on a close relationship with DPVR, a global VR HMD manufacturer. The most crucial factor in the VR market is still the distribution channel. While ComixV boasts overwhelming accessibility compared to other VR content due to its advantage of supporting both web and mobile, it's difficult to claim superiority in accessibility when compared to the competitiveness of mobile webtoons, which are snack culture.

{{IMG:1}}

However, ComixV is a startup targeting the upcoming VR/AR market. Therefore, securing the most important customer touchpoints in the current VR market is crucial. In that regard, VR platforms that directly connect with users, such as HMD manufacturers, mobile carriers, and LVEs, are inevitably very important channels.

**ComixV Targets China in Collaboration with Haotoon... To Offer Original Webtoons and Various Webtoon Chinese Services** ComixV, which offers global VR webtoon services, announced that it launched a platform service directly targeting the Chinese local market in collaboration with Haotoon on the 15th. It will be first unveiled to Chinese consumers starting on the 19th at the '2019 World VR Industry Conference' held in Nanchang, China. In addition to over 100 episodes of original VR webtoons directly planned and produced, original works from Korean webtoon companies such as Daum Webtoon, Manhwagajok, and Storysoop will be converted into VR webtoons and presented in Chinese. ComixV stated, "This platform launch is a global VR HMD (Head Mo... webtooninsight.co.kr

https://webtooninsight.co.kr/Forum/Content/6448

Just as the early webtoon market was content aimed at attracting users to portal platforms, VR platforms need VR webtoons. This entry into China also began with the requirement to properly support the infrastructure within China. The launch in the Chinese market was a big challenge for ComixV. We implemented a scalable system and synchronization between multiple regions (though it's not yet perfect). Starting with D'Live's requirements, we implemented an API configuration that supports multiple versions for content distribution across DPVR and various channels, and resolved chronic file issues by implementing the API, which was previously built with MS Azure Blob's file system, as a memory cache. Many people, when thinking of a platform, easily assume it's a content distribution platform that's done once developed. However, in reality, backend development issues account for a larger portion than the visible changes. For this, having an effective development structure is also the biggest concern for platforms like ComixV. The platform created through this opportunity also aimed to build a foundation to open up the VR webtoon upload system for artists in VR illustration to users. For this, we completed the development of a distributed processing structure for 360 images two months ago. While there are still many stabilization issues and remaining development tasks, we can say that we have at least laid the basic structure for user expansion.

In terms of VR webtoon content supply and distribution, we established a conversion system overseas, and in addition to direct contracts with artists, which we've been doing, we also signed secondary copyright agreements with agencies including Daum Webtoon. As there were no precedents, contract review took more time than expected. Later, we will need to consider how to create a standard contract by comparing all these agreements. I also believe that the value of the VR webtoon market will be determined by how the contracts are structured. It seems necessary to create a structure that clearly communicates that VR webtoons are a market that expands in scale without competing with the existing webtoon market.

The platform's launch is not the end, but the beginning of the battle. This is because sustainability is the most important thing. It would be a lie to say it's not difficult. However, hearing the stories of those who support us gives me strength and makes me want to push further. And no matter what anyone says, we will advance little by little at our own pace, mobilizing the resources we have. In these times when we hear so many stories about difficulties, I hope ComixV can leave a footprint that is at least somewhat helpful to the VR industry and the comic industry. The exhibition must be in full swing in Nanchang right now... I couldn't go due to work, but my heart is there.

