I've introduced Yper service on my homepage a few times, and now I've had the chance to experience it myself, so I'd like to share it with you. Yper is a leading O2O company in Korea, introduced overseas, and has swept various awards, making other startups envious. Simply put, O2O refers to a service model where offline services, or services used in daily life, are accessed and received at a desired location using online services like smartphones. Yper falls into this type of service because they come to your location to pick up your car.

{{IMG:1}}

A characteristic of O2O services is that while you apply for and access them online, the actual service takes place offline. This often leads to limitations in service areas. Yper's only drawback was that its service was limited to the Gangnam area, where its partner companies are concentrated. It's very welcome news that it's recently expanded to Pangyo. After praising Yper's service, I was given the opportunity to try it myself, so I'm writing a review of my direct experience.

First, since my workplace is in Pangyo, I don't go to Gangnam regularly. However, due to frequent meetings, I often had to go to Gangnam. So, I booked Yper the day before my meeting. There are several types of car washes available.

{{IMG:2}}

{{IMG:3}}

{{IMG:4}}

I simply chose the Basic Plus, and then selected my desired time and location through the app, and the reservation was complete.

{{IMG:5}}

The next morning

{{IMG:6}}

https://blog.naver.com/PostView.naver?blogId=fstory97&logNo=223833997537&redirect=Dlog&widgetTypeCall=true&noTrackingCode=true&directAccess=false#

Leaves falling everywhere... It's okay. Because today I'm getting a car wash.

{{IMG:7}}

That day, before my meeting, I received a call from the manager, and handsome individuals arrived in blue uniforms.

{{IMG:8}}

But two car seats... Sorry. Felt unnecessarily apologetic.

{{IMG:9}}

They meticulously checked the car's condition in case of any potential disputes.

{{IMG:10}}

And the handover receipt. It also includes insurance, so I felt at ease. It happens very quickly, and here's a tip: most parking lots in Gangnam offer free exit within 30 minutes.

After the meeting, a phone call came, and the car was supposed to arrive.... Non-existent image. It was supposed to come... but when I used it, the map function was still lacking. News broke yesterday that they signed an MOU with a location specialist company, so I believe they will now provide location tracking like Kakao Taxi. And voilà... the new car arrived.

{{IMG:11}}

Ta-da! New car arrived. Looking at its condition, it was quite meticulously done.

And here's a tip: if you adjust the car pick-up time to when your work ends, you can save on parking fees. Save parking fees while getting your car washed during work in Gangnam.

And after driving the car for a few days, I noticed that even though it's an outdoor apartment parking lot and cars get dirty easily, the shine lasted for quite a long time. I realized, "This is the beauty of a hand car wash." Seeing my sparkling car among other dirty ones gives me an inexplicable sense of satisfaction.

Since it's coming to Pangyo soon, I should use it occasionally, even if not every time.

Currently, Korean manufacturers refer to devices in the following form as TVs.

{{IMG:1}}

However, Apple calls this a TV.

{{IMG:2}}

What is the difference? Now, one more thing... Should this be called a TV?

{{IMG:3}}

Recently, large monitors have built-in HDMI ports and speakers, allowing them to be used as TVs by connecting an IPTV set-top box. What exactly is a "real TV" that we talk about? I used to work in the smart TV sector. Manufacturers, having been burned by the iPhone craze, feared that TVs would also fall behind Apple. The biggest fear was that Samsung Electronics and LG Electronics would be relegated from finished product manufacturers of TVs to mere display manufacturers. Relatively large displays have a long replacement cycle, whereas set-top boxes that support smart TV functions have a short replacement cycle. Because of these concerns, LG Electronics released a separate set-top box model, and Samsung Electronics released an upgrade kit called the Evolution Kit. After that, smart TVs didn't really create much buzz. More importantly, broadcast consumption in Korea was centered around IPTV, so the TV content domain had already shifted to mobile carriers. It seemed that smart TVs were being sold, but no one was using them smartly. In the midst of this, Xiaomi released the Mi TV 3.

{{IMG:4}}

It supports a 60-inch 4K resolution with an LG display (presumably IPS) and was released at an absurd price of 880,000 won. But what surprised me about this product wasn't just that.

{{IMG:5}}

The TV no longer has any ports; all of the Mi TV's ports have moved to the soundbar. This changes TV upgrades to upgrading the soundbar and display separately. Not only is it cheap, but I think it's very realistic and innovative. However, a device similar to this innovative model already existed in Korea: the TVG Woofer, which is serviced by Uplus.

{{IMG:6}}

The problem was that even if you used this, you still had to buy a TV with redundant features like smart TV or 3D TV. In fact, TV manufacturers were aware of this problem and disliked it. As almost all core TV functions moved to set-top boxes like IPTV, the role of the TV was changing to that of a simple monitor. If that happened, the role of the TV would be greatly diminished, and it wouldn't be able to capture a larger share than just a panel production component supplier. However, Xiaomi tackled this head-on by separating them instead of bundling them. This falls within my definition of innovation. It's not just about superior functionality, but because the business model itself changes. Existing manufacturers were aware of this trend, but it was a very burdensome model to attempt. This is because as soon as they create such a model, they would have to give up their advantages as a finished TV manufacturer and simultaneously confront smart set-top boxes like Apple TV. The Mi TV uses Samsung and LG panels. This highlights the status of Samsung and LG Electronics. It means that Korean manufacturers are already being relegated to component suppliers. Of course, if Mi TVs sell well, it would also benefit Samsung and LG Electronics, but ultimately, the finished product manufacturer is at the user's touchpoint, and they would have no influence over software or services. I think this product starkly illustrates the current situation of Korean manufacturers. While Korean manufacturers are still powerful as component manufacturers, they seem to be rapidly losing ground to China in the finished product sector. The finished product company, which delivers the final product to consumers, ultimately determines the price and which component manufacturers to deal with. If they pursue technological internalization, they can even produce components themselves. Chinese companies are already attracting that much capital and talent. Korean manufacturers, who once dominated the global market with cheaper prices than Japan and decent technology, are now giving way to powerful and massive manufacturers that combine very cheap prices, technology that has absorbed top talent worldwide, and China's enormous capital. I even feel that there is very little time left for Korean manufacturing in Korea. And the collapse of manufacturing, which has supported the Korean economy, will pose a major crisis for the Korean economy. Of course, that doesn't mean we should absolutely pour everything into fostering Korean manufacturing. Korean manufacturing grew because the timing was right. If the timing isn't right, it's not a situation where the people of a small country can push it to succeed in the global market. Instead, it's likely to be like pouring water into a bottomless pit. What is needed now is only innovation. And that innovation comes with pain and requires the destruction of existing paradigms. Now, a TV is a set-top box with built-in software, and the large screen we see on the outside is just a hardware display. This signifies the end of the hardware-centric TV era. Xiaomi positions itself as a software company.

Do you know Kids Note, where memories from 20,000 Korean daycare centers and kindergartens accumulate? It's a service that was recently acquired by Daum Kakao, famous for KakaoTalk. It's reportedly used in a whopping 40% of daycare centers and kindergartens nationwide. As accidents in daycare centers and kindergartens have been continuously reported recently, parents are often anxious. However, for centers using Kids Note, activity logs are regularly sent with photos or videos, and comments can be added to these posts, making it easy to communicate with the center and its teachers, or check medication requests and meal plans. Since my son's daycare also uses Kids Note, I receive daily push notifications. Sometimes, while working, I get a push notification and feel happy seeing that my son is doing well.
{{IMG:1}}I don't know why he thought the toilet was his dad's ㅜㅜ
Anyway, Kids Note has launched a service called Storybook, which I'd like to introduce. It's impossible to continuously offer such a great service for free, right? You can't run a business by digging holes in the ground... Kids Note Storybook is a service that turns the photos accumulated in Kids Note into an album. It's a real album, not an online one. I was lucky enough to be selected for the experience group, so I'm leaving this review.
First, what arrived looked like this...{{IMG:2}}

The cover is matte, with a slight drawing paper feel, so it doesn't quite give off a very luxurious impression.
{{IMG:3}}

Let me show you my son's face up close first. {{IMG:4}}
Turning the page, first, a summary... number of photos, videos, comments, posts... Honestly, I'm not sure what meaning this has in an album.
{{IMG:5}}

Next is the table of contents, organized by month.
{{IMG:6}}

It's grouped in 1-month units.
{{IMG:7}}

The internal content structure is roughly like this: a mobile screen, which is usually scrollable, divided into two columns and arranged vertically.
{{IMG:8}}

Videos have QR codes embedded. You can use portal apps like Naver App or Daum App, or a dedicated reader app for QR codes. Please note that Kids Note does not have a QR code reader function. I suspect it might be integrated into KakaoTalk soon since search has been added there, but I'll discuss that in a future post.
{{IMG:9}}

Oh... it won't scan ㅜㅜ The QR code is misaligned. This is presumed to be a bug where a shortened URL wasn't created. Don't know what a shortened URL is? You probably don't need to worry about it, as they'll likely fix it soon. {{IMG:10}}
Anyway, if there's an advantage, it's that memories compiled into a book are great to look through all at once. The actual service has now launched.
To find it, tap 'Photo Store' in 'More', then select 'Kids Note Storybook'. There's also 'Snaps Photo Store', but I haven't tried that yet.{{IMG:11}} {{IMG:12}}
Select a child. Select a theme, select what content to create it with. {{IMG:13}} {{IMG:14}}
Then, wait, and ta-da... {{IMG:15}} {{IMG:16}}
If you'd like to see a sample yourself, check out the URL below. You can also create a sample and share it.
[http://kn.bollogbook.com/html/preview/preview_m.html#201507162211576211](http://kn.bollogbook.com/html/preview/preview_m.html#201507162211576211)

The most important thing: price! Around 300 pages costs 93,000 won... Based on my experience, I got 160 pages for about 3.5 months. If I had paid the actual price, it would have been around 60,000 won. Calculating at about 45 pages per month, that's 540 pages for a year. Approximately 167,000 won... It's a bit more expensive than I thought.
{{IMG:17}} I wonder if it wouldn't be better to offer discounts for monthly or quarterly subscriptions, or even better, allow daycare centers and kindergartens to customize them, adding more of their unique character, and then sell them directly. If daycare centers could customize them for 15,000 to 20,000 won per month and sell them mixed in with tuition fees, I think it would be hard for parents not to buy them.

**ps. This post was written after being selected as part of the Storybook experience group and receiving a Storybook.**

