**2014. 05. 05**
At that time, I was working part-time at a PC bang. Back then, StarCraft 1 and Diablo 2 were hugely popular, and if there was a representative image of that era, it would probably be the PC bang. There were quite a few customers who would just run off without paying, so the person who woke up a dozing part-timer, paid, and left must have been a very conscientious person. If there were more people like that in the world, wouldn't it be a much better place than it is now?

Last Friday, I attended Planet X hosted by SK Planet. It was an event similar to a hackathon, and I'd like to introduce it to you. [http://www.planetx.co.kr/](http://www.planetx.co.kr/)

**1. SK, which had been consistent with control and closure, now talks about openness and co-prosperity.** Until now, Cyworld's failure and TStore in the feature phone era were synonymous with control and closure. This SK has reportedly created SK Planet to gather group-level services and open them up to a unified platform level. {{IMG:1}} {{IMG:2}} As part of this event, an awards ceremony for the Code Sprint programming competition was held during the first session last Friday. This event is expected to take place annually. It was clear that this event, as a pure programming and algorithm-based competition rather than a typical contest, was more focused on developers. **2. What is being opened?** The roadmap for Planet X is largely drawn around well-known services and backend features necessary for developers. {{IMG:3}} **2.1) One ID** Although it can be classified as a backend feature, I've given it a separate number because it's the entry point for all services. First, in integrating many of SK's services, a unified SK ID has been created. This is a natural issue that arises when unifying the access points for services that were previously separate. The problem is that users will have to switch to One ID, and the security incident at SK Comms will likely make users' trust in SK's security a hurdle. In fact, Naver already offers Naver login using oAuth. In Naver's case, it plans to support simple login (one-click) like its own apps, so it's hard to see SK's offering as having a strong advantage over Naver. Since global SWs use Facebook, it seems difficult to find the distinctiveness of SK's OneID. **2.2) Service** SK is opening up its various services. **- TMap** Tmap, the undisputed No. 1 navigation app with powerful real-time traffic information as its weapon, is opening some of its APIs. The problem is that it's not a global map, so apps aiming for global expansion are unlikely to choose it. Me2day uses Naver Maps domestically and Google Maps internationally. You can think of it in a similar context. TMap's competitiveness lies in navigation, but its domestic POI (Points of Interest) competitiveness is lower than Naver's, and its international competitiveness is lower than Google Maps, so there doesn't seem to be a strong reason for developers to choose it. If there's an advantage, it would be the accuracy of location information, which is a strength of mobile carriers. **- Cyworld/NateOn** Cyworld is in a groggy state, having already ceded leadership to Facebook and mobile dominance to KakaoStory due to its belated openness. Its strength lies in being the only one of the top three portals to have a social graph. However, the problem is that Cyworld's own usage is declining. It has even fallen behind Naver Games in the social gaming sector. A turnaround in this area is needed for API demand to grow. **- 11st** You can view product and purchase information. I'm not sure what can be created with this. **- TCloud** This is a service positioned like Dropbox. The problem with TCloud is that if you're not an SKT subscriber, the storage limit becomes much smaller. Therefore, unless you're an SKT user, it's also a problem for global use. It's unclear how much its usage compares to NDrive. If it connects with SKB's Smart TV, it seems to have potential. **- Hoppin** Almost identical to TCloud. Could it become YouTube? **2.3) Backend Feature** **- NFC/Smart Touch** Opens USIM-based payment APIs. As the most powerful resource of mobile carriers, mobile payment, if merchant expansion occurs, it could become a very meaningful service. This is a difficult area for portals or other services to follow, and direct contact with infrastructure and retailers seems to be the biggest strength. **- Push** The strongest advantage of mobile carriers is Push. While Google and Apple have usage restrictions and cannot guarantee real-time delivery, the strength of mobile carriers in the network sector cannot be denied. **- Community/Comments (Apocato)** Provides a real-time comment system. It's a powerful comment service that works similarly to social comments like LiveRe, clearly showing user reactions and recommended comments while they are being written. Unlike open source, all comments are stored on SK's servers, so I wonder if SKP might create new services with this in the future. **- Culuu** A PaaS service that allows you to set up servers and services with simple clicks. Compared to AWS or KT's uCloudBiz, it's much simpler and characterized by a simple approach to commonly used positions. It's also designed to easily migrate to other PaaS services, not just Culuu, in the future. It hasn't officially launched yet, and a paid billing model doesn't seem to be in place... Its simplicity stood out. **3. Hoping for a virtuous cycle of openness and co-prosperity** The method of opening a platform involves spreading the platform's own competitiveness externally, and then external usage helps to grow the platform's competitiveness again, creating a virtuous cycle. If the platform itself is competitive, it's easy for more external users to use it and create this virtuous cycle. But if not, policies to encourage more external use or efforts to enhance the platform's competitiveness are needed to create that cycle. While SK's influence has weakened significantly due to the rise of service platforms like Kakao and Naver, its advantages as a platform operator with both infrastructure and services cannot be denied. And this powerful platform operator has begun to open up with determination. Seeing them provide expensive meals and performances, they certainly seem determined. ^^ I hope for good results. I expect these good attempts to become the foundation for IT developers and ventures. Can we look forward to a changed SK? **ps.** The answer to the PlanetX quiz was the 10th planet of the solar system... which is incorrect information. In 2006, Pluto was reclassified as a dwarf planet, making Planet X the 9th hypothetical planet. [http://ko.wikipedia.org/wiki/%ED%96%89%EC%84%B1_X](http://ko.wikipedia.org/wiki/%ED%96%89%EC%84%B1_X) I declare that this posting has not received any separate compensation. [재생하기 바로보기가 지원되지 않는 파일입니다. 클릭하여 팝업창으로 플레이 해보세요.](http://blog.naver.com/EmbedHttpView.naver?blogId=fstory97&logNo=70151730563&hashKey=940760d75417a978a8243883b6bd884a) **********http://www.hanrss.com/add_sub.qst?url=http%3A%2F%2Fblog.rss.naver.com%2Ffstory97.xml**** {{IMG:4}}

